SYSTEM DESIGN INTERVIEW – VOLUME 4

Global AI Systems & LLM Platforms
===================================


Page 1 — Introduction

Large-scale AI platforms combine distributed systems, GPU orchestration, massive data pipelines, and retrieval infrastructure. Unlike traditional system design, AI systems include unique constraints: model weights, token throughput, GPU memory, batching, KV cache, embeddings, and latency guarantees across regions.

This volume focuses on the architecture behind global LLM platforms similar to:

OpenAI ChatGPT
Anthropic Claude
Google Gemini
AWS Bedrock
Meta LlamaStack

Page 2 — The AI System Design Mindset

Designing AI systems requires balancing:

Model constraints
Weights are huge (2–400GB)
GPU memory is limited (40–80GB typically)
KV-cache increases linearly with prompt length
Infrastructure constraints
GPU scarcity
Multi-region placement
Network bandwidth for retrieval
Latency constraints
Token generation must begin < 200ms
Streaming must feel natural
Cost constraints
GPU-hour is expensive
Batch sizes impact utilization
Model distillation reduces cost

Page 3 — High-Level Global AI Platform (Bird’s Eye View)

A global AI platform typically contains:

LLM Inference Cluster (GPU)
Embedding Service
Vector DB or Hybrid Search
Orchestration Layer (router + load balancer)
RAG Pipeline
Safety & Moderation Stack
User Data Store
Observability & Telemetry
Multi-region DR / failover

Key challenge: Everything must work across regions reliably.

Page 4 — LLM Inference Pipeline Overview

The typical inference steps:

Request enters global load balancer
Tenant routing & auth
Prompt preprocessing
Tokenization
Truncation
Context normalization
Select model version & region
KV-cache warm-up / reuse
GPU inference execution
Token streaming back to client
Post-processing
Logging & safety checks

This pipeline must operate at scale: millions of daily requests.

Page 5 — GPU Cluster Architecture

GPU clusters are usually grouped:

Inference nodes (A100/H100)
Inference pods (8–16 GPUs)
High-speed interconnect—NVLink / InfiniBand
Model Parallelism:
Tensor parallel
Pipeline parallel
Fully-sharded data parallel

Goal: Maximize GPU utilization. GPU idle time = wasted $$.

Techniques used:

Request batching
Speculative decoding
FlashAttention
KV cache compression
Page 6 — KV Cache & Token Throughput

The KV cache stores attention keys/values for each generated token.

Long prompts → huge KV memory
Larger models → more KV per token
Multi-user batching → increased reuse opportunities

If a model has:

70B parameters
Generates 100 tokens
With 8192 context window

KV cache can exceed 10–20GB per session.

This is why LLMs scale differently from normal stateless services.

Page 7 — Multi-Region Architecture

AI systems must operate across:

US East
US West
Europe
Asia
Middle East

Key features:

Distributed vector indexes
Region-local inference clusters
Async replication
Failover strategies
Model weight distribution via blob storage

Hardest challenge:
Keeping embeddings + indexes + safety classifiers + model snapshots consistent across regions.

Page 8 — Vector Database Architecture

Global vector DBs (Pinecone-like, FAISS-based, or Qdrant-like) support:

Sharding (by docID, tenant, or embedding hash)
HNSW graphs
Product Quantization
Hybrid search (sparse + dense)
Tiered storage

Design challenges:

Cross-region replication
Consistency vs latency
Index rebuilds vs live ingestion
Query routing

For global RAG, index locality is critical.

Page 9 — RAG Pipeline Overview

A full RAG system includes:

Document ingestion
Chunking & embedding
Vector storage
Query embedding
Retriever
Ranker (cross-encoder)
Context selection
LLM synthesis

Common architectures:

Classic RAG (single pass)
Reranked RAG
Fusion architectures
Graph-RAG / Knowledge Graph RAG
Agentic RAG with tools
Page 10 — Safety & Guardrail Layer

Global AI platforms must implement:

Prompt filtering
Toxicity classifier
PII detection
Hallucination risk scoring
Output moderation
Red teaming rulesets
Tenant-tier policies

Moderation is often a separate model that runs before and after the LLM.
